var e=1,t=`der-forwards-01`,n=`derivatives`,r=`Forwards and Futures: The Cost of Carry`,i=`A futures price is today's price plus the cost of holding the thing until delivery — not a forecast of what it will cost then.`,a=`core`,o=[`quant-tvm-01`],s=9,c=[`CFA-L1`,`CAIA-L1`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`A **[[forward-contract|forward]]** is an agreement to buy or sell something at a fixed price on a future date. A **[[futures-contract|future]]** is the same economic bargain, standardised and traded on an exchange with daily margining.

The differences matter operationally rather than economically:

**A forward** is bilateral and customisable, settles once at maturity, and carries counterparty risk.

**A future** is exchange-traded, standardised, marked to market daily with cash flowing between the parties, and the exchange stands between you and your counterparty.

**That daily settlement is the practical difference that catches people out.** A forward that moves against you costs nothing until maturity. A future that moves against you demands cash tomorrow morning — which is why hedging programmes fail on liquidity rather than on logic.

And the pricing principle, which is the whole subject: **the forward price is set by arbitrage, not by expectation.** If it were higher, you could buy the asset today, sell it forward, and pocket the difference less the cost of holding it. That trade is what pins the price.`},{type:`concept`,id:`cost-of-carry`,body:`**The forward price equals the spot price plus everything it costs to hold the asset until delivery, minus everything it pays you while you hold it.**

The components differ by asset:

**Financing.** You must fund the purchase, so the interest cost is added. Always present.

**Storage and insurance.** Physical commodities cost money to keep. Added.

**Income.** Dividends on an equity index, coupons on a bond. Subtracted, because holding the asset pays you.

**[[convenience-yield|Convenience yield]].** The non-financial benefit of physically holding a commodity when supply is tight — a refinery values having oil rather than a claim on oil. Subtracted, and it is the term that makes commodity curves interesting.

So a commodity at 100 with 5% funding and 2% storage has a one-year forward of **107** — a curve in [[contango]], sloping upward.

The same commodity with an 8% convenience yield has a forward of **99** — [[backwardation]], sloping downward. **The market is paying you to defer delivery, because holding the physical right now is worth more than the money.**

**Which is why backwardation is a statement about physical scarcity rather than a forecast of falling prices.** It is one of the most misread signals in commodity markets.`},{type:`intuition`,id:`roll-yield`,body:`**A futures position held for more than one contract's life earns or loses money purely from the shape of the curve, before the price does anything.**

The mechanism: futures expire, so a continuous exposure requires selling the expiring contract and buying a later one. That is the roll, and the shape of the curve determines whether it costs or pays.

**In contango** — later contracts more expensive — you sell low and buy high at each roll. **Negative [[roll-yield|roll yield]]**, and it compounds. This is why long commodity index positions in persistently contangoed markets have lost money while the spot price rose.

**In backwardation** you sell high and buy low. **Positive roll yield**, earned continuously with no price move at all.

The practical consequences are large:

**A commodity fund's return has three parts** — spot price change, roll yield, and the interest earned on the collateral. Attribution that shows only the first is missing most of the story.

**"Commodity exposure" is not one thing.** An index rolling monthly through a contangoed curve is a different investment from one holding physical or from a producer's equity.

**And the roll cost is knowable in advance.** The curve is observable, so the drag can be computed before the position is taken — which makes it a decision rather than a surprise.`},{type:`formula`,id:`carry-formula`,latex:`F = S \\times (1 + r + c - y)^T`,plainReading:`The forward price is the spot price grown at the financing rate, plus storage costs, minus any income or convenience yield, compounded over the time to delivery.`,variables:[{symbol:`S`,meaning:`spot price today`},{symbol:`r`,meaning:`financing rate — the cost of funding the purchase`},{symbol:`c`,meaning:`storage and insurance, for physical assets`},{symbol:`y`,meaning:`income or [[convenience-yield|convenience yield]] received from holding it`}]},{type:`example`,id:`worked-carry`,body:`A commodity trades at 100. Funding costs 5% a year, storage 2%. Separately, an equity index at 100 with a 3% dividend yield and the same 5% funding rate.`,walkthrough:[`**Commodity, no convenience yield.** Forward = 100 × (1 + 0.05 + 0.02) = **107.00**. The curve is in contango.`,`**Equity index.** Forward = 100 × (1 + 0.05 − 0.03) = **102.00**. Financing costs more than the dividend pays, so the forward sits above spot — but by less.`,`**Commodity with an 8% convenience yield.** Forward = 100 × (1 + 0.05 + 0.02 − 0.08) = **99.00**. Backwardation.`,`**Read that carefully: the same commodity at the same spot price has a forward above or below spot depending entirely on whether physical holders currently value having the material.** Nobody forecast anything.`,`Now the roll. Holding the contangoed commodity for a year through twelve monthly rolls, with the curve unchanged, costs roughly the 7% of carry — you buy each new contract above where you sold the last.`,`Against that, the backwardated market pays roughly 1% a year for the same passive exposure.`,`Sanity check worth reusing: **before taking any futures exposure, compute the annualised roll cost from the observable curve.** It is knowable in advance, it is often larger than the expected price move, and it is the number most commonly left out of a commodity allocation case.`]},{type:`table`,id:`carry-table`,caption:`What drives the curve for each asset`,headers:[`Asset`,`Curve normally`,`Because`],rows:[[`Equity index`,`Slightly upward`,`Financing exceeds the dividend yield, so carry is a small positive cost.`],[`Government bond`,`Depends on the curve`,`Coupon income against financing — positive carry when the curve is upward sloping.`],[`Storable commodity, ample supply`,`[[contango|Contango]]`,`Financing plus storage, with little convenience value in holding physical.`],[`Commodity in shortage`,`[[backwardation|Backwardation]]`,`Convenience yield exceeds carry — physical possession is worth a premium now.`],[`Currency`,`Set by the rate differential`,`[[covered-interest-parity|Covered interest parity]] — the same arbitrage in a different market.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Reading the curve as a forecast.** A futures curve in contango does not predict rising prices; it reflects carry. Historically, futures curves have been poor forecasters — which is precisely why roll returns exist as a systematic source of gain and loss.

**Ignoring roll cost in a long-horizon position.** A 7% annual roll drag on a commodity index means the spot price must rise 7% a year for a flat return. Over five years that is a very large hurdle.

**Assuming a hedge is free.** Selling futures against a physical position locks the price and hands over the roll. In backwardation, that hedge costs the positive carry you would otherwise have earned.

**[[margin-call|Margin calls]] on a winning hedge.** Futures settle daily. A hedge that is working — because the asset it protects has risen — pays out cash daily against an unrealised gain.

**Forgetting the contract specification.** Grade, delivery location and delivery month all differ between the contract and the exposure. That gap is basis risk, and it has its own topic.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`carry-in-alts`,body:`**Carry appears everywhere in alternatives once you know to look for it, and it is usually the part of a return that gets attributed to skill.**

**[[managed-futures|Managed futures]] and commodity strategies** earn or pay roll yield continuously. A trend-following programme in backwardated markets has a tailwind that has nothing to do with the trend signal.

**Currency hedging** is the same arbitrage: the hedge cost is the rate differential, computable in advance.

**Financing spreads in relative value.** A [[fixed-income-relative-value|fixed income relative value]] position financed in [[repo]] earns the carry between the bond's coupon and the repo rate — often most of the return, with the convergence trade as the option on top.

**Cash-and-carry arbitrage.** Buying spot and selling forward when the forward is above fair carry. Real, competitive and thin.

**The general question worth asking of any strategy: how much of the return is carry that would accrue without any decision being made?** Carry is a legitimate and durable source of return — it is compensation for financing, storage or providing liquidity. **It is not a forecasting skill, and a track record earned in a favourable carry environment will not repeat in an unfavourable one.**`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Compute the annualised roll cost from the curve before any futures-based exposure.** It is observable and frequently larger than the expected price move.

**Ask a commodity manager to decompose returns into spot, roll and collateral.** Three components, and only one of them is a price view.

**For any futures hedge, ask where the margin cash comes from in the scenario where the hedge loses.** That is the scenario where the underlying is doing well and you have no cash from it.

**Ask whether the contract matches the exposure** — grade, location, month. Mismatches are basis risk, and they are chosen at the outset.

**Treat backwardation as information about physical tightness, not as a price forecast.** It tells you the market values immediate delivery, which is a supply statement rather than a prediction.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`A forward price is spot plus carry — financing and storage, less income and convenience yield. Arbitrage sets it, not expectation.`,`The same 100 commodity has a forward of 107.00 in contango and 99.00 with an 8% convenience yield. Backwardation is a statement about physical scarcity, not a price forecast.`,`Rolling futures earns or loses money from the curve's shape alone: contango costs, backwardation pays, and the drag is computable in advance.`,`Ask any commodity strategy to split returns into spot, roll and collateral. Carry is a real and durable return source, and it is not forecasting skill.`]}],u=[{id:`der-forwards-01-q1`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:2,tags:[`forwards`,`carry`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-carry`,stem:`A commodity trades at 100. Funding is 5% and storage 2%, with no convenience yield. What is the one-year forward price?`,answer:107,tolerance:.5,toleranceType:`abs`,inputHint:`Spot grown by financing plus storage.`,explanation:`This is contango, and it is the normal state for a storable commodity in ample supply. Note that nothing here is a forecast — the 107 would hold even if everyone expected the price to fall, because the arbitrage does not care about opinions.`},{id:`der-forwards-01-q2`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`forwards`,`carry`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-carry`,stem:`An equity index trades at 100 with a 3% dividend yield. Funding is 5%. What is the one-year forward price?`,answer:102,tolerance:.5,toleranceType:`abs`,explanation:`Income is subtracted because holding the asset pays you. If the dividend yield exceeded the financing rate the forward would sit below spot — which happens in high-dividend markets when rates are low, and surprises people who expect forwards always to be higher.`},{id:`der-forwards-01-q3`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`forwards`,`backwardation`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-carry`,stem:`Same commodity at 100, 5% funding, 2% storage, but now an 8% convenience yield. What is the one-year forward price?`,answer:99,tolerance:.5,toleranceType:`abs`,explanation:`Backwardation, and the cause is physical: someone needs the material now and will pay for immediate possession. It is a supply signal rather than a bearish price forecast, and reading it as the latter is one of the most common errors in commodity markets.`},{id:`der-forwards-01-q4`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`forwards`,`roll-yield`],concept:`roll-yield`,stem:`A commodity index rolls monthly through a persistently contangoed curve. What happens to returns?`,choices:[`Returns match the spot price change`,`Returns lag the spot price change, because each roll sells a cheaper contract and buys a more expensive one`,`Returns exceed the spot price change`,`Roll has no effect on returns`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`They only match if the curve is flat, which is unusual and not the case in contango.`,`Correct. Each roll crystallises the carry cost. Over a year in a 7% contango, that is a 7% drag before the spot price does anything.`,`That is the backwardation case, where the roll pays.`,`The roll is a real transaction at real prices, and in a steep curve it dominates the return.`],explanation:`This explains a result that surprises people regularly: long commodity index positions losing money over periods when the spot commodity rose. The spot went up and the investor was paying carry the whole way, and the carry was larger.`},{id:`der-forwards-01-q5`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`forwards`,`misconception`],concept:`cost-of-carry`,stem:`"The oil futures curve is in backwardation, so the market expects prices to fall." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — backwardation means the convenience yield exceeds carry, which is a statement about current physical tightness rather than a price forecast`,`False — backwardation means the market expects prices to rise`,`True — a downward-sloping curve is the market's price forecast`,`False — because oil futures are always in contango`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. Someone needs the physical material now and will pay a premium for immediate delivery. The curve reflects the cost and benefit of holding, not a prediction.`,`The curve is not an expectation in either direction — that is the point being tested.`,`This is the intuitive reading and it is the error. Curves are arbitrage relationships.`,`Oil moves between contango and backwardation depending on inventories, sometimes within months.`],explanation:`The same principle covers currency forwards and implied forward interest rates: all three are no-arbitrage prices routinely misread as forecasts. Once you see it in one market the pattern is unmistakable in the others.`},{id:`der-forwards-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`forwards`,`attribution`,`on-the-job`],concept:`carry-in-alts`,stem:`A managed futures fund reports strong returns in a period of widespread backwardation. What should you check?`,choices:[`Whether the fund used leverage`,`How much of the return came from roll yield rather than from the trend signals themselves`,`The fund's Sharpe ratio`,`The number of markets traded`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Leverage scales the return and does not separate its sources.`,`Correct. Backwardation pays a long position continuously, independent of any trend-following signal. That tailwind vanishes when curves flip to contango, and the track record will not repeat.`,`The Sharpe ratio summarises the outcome without identifying what produced it.`,`Breadth affects diversification, not the source of the return.`],explanation:`The generalisable habit is to ask what a strategy would have earned doing nothing but holding its typical exposure through that period. If most of the return was available passively, the manager was well positioned rather than skilful — and the position is observable in the curve.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
